    "evidence_text": "DNA Deamination Mediates Innate Immunity to Retroviral Infection\n\nRNase H and Postreplication Repair Protect Cells from Ribonucleotides Incorporated in DNA\n\nMisincorporation is limited by the selectivity of DNA replicases.\n\nThe cytidine deaminase CEM15 induces hypermutation in newly synthesized HIV-1 DNA\n\nHigh mutation frequency during reverse transcription has a principal role in the genetic variation of primate lentiviral populations.\n\nIt is the main driving force for the generation of drug resistance and the escape from immune surveillance.\n\nThe molecular mechanisms of this process, however, remain to be clarified.\n\nThis effect can be counteracted by the HIV-1 virion infectivity factor (Vif).\n\nBroad antiretroviral defence by human APOBEC3G through lethal editing of nascent reverse transcripts\n\nViral replication usually requires that innate intracellular lines of defence be overcome, a task usually accomplished by specialized viral gene products.\n\nWhen produced in the presence of APOBEC3G, vif-defective virus is non-infectious.\n\nThis site is easily and efficiently retargeted to other cellular and viral miRNAs by target site replacement.\n\nAnalysis of human cytomegalovirus-encoded microRNA activity during infection.\n\nVirally encoded miRNAs are unique in that they could potentially target both viral and host genes.\n\nStress-induced apoptosis associated with null mutation of ADAR1 RNA editing deaminase gene.\n\nA-to-I RNA editing of the coding sequence could result in synthesis of proteins not directly encoded in the genome.\n\nThree mammalian ADAR gene family members (ADAR1-3) have been identified.\n\nHere we investigated phenotypes of mice homozygous for ADAR1 null mutation.\n\nCDE-1 Affects Chromosome Segregation through Uridylation of CSR-1-Bound siRNAs\n\nCDE-1 localizes specifically to mitotic chromosomes in embryos.\n\nHelicobacter pylori infection triggers aberrant expression of activation-induced cytidine deaminase in gastric epithelium\n\nDeficiency in l-serine deaminase results in abnormal growth and cell division of Escherichia coli K-12.",
